    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$4.02 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a chop-and-dirty-rice plate that a Cajun restaurant would price at $16. Two weeks of dinners out of one pot. Laissez les bons temps rouler, cheaply.",
    "shop_smart":  ["Pork chops are the anchor here, and the 6 pound pack keeps the per-pound price down. When chops hit the sale board, this recipe gets even cheaper per bowl.","One pound of chorizo seasons the entire rice pot. It is one of the highest flavor-per-dollar items in the meat case.","Cooking the rice in chicken broth instead of water costs just a little more and is the difference between rice and dirty rice. One of the cheapest upgrades in the recipe."],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cook the rice in the chicken broth (3.5 cups dry rice in about half the broth), then set aside. Slightly firm is perfect since it cooks again.",
                    "Brown the chorizo in your big pot over medium-high, breaking it up, until it renders and crisps in spots.",
                    "Add the diced onion, red bell pepper, and garlic and cook 5 minutes until soft. Stir in the thyme and a spoon of the cajun seasoning.",
                    "Fold in the cooked rice and a splash of remaining broth, scraping the bottom, until every grain turns the color dirty rice is named for. Finish with green onions.",
                    "Pat the chops dry, season hard with the rest of the cajun seasoning, salt, and pepper, and sear in olive oil 3 to 4 minutes per side until they reach 145 F.",
                    "Rest the chops 5 minutes, slice, and serve over the dirty rice."
                ],
